var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"borel/#Borel-plane","page":"Borel plane","text":"borel and inverse_borel are exact, in rising-factorial normalization. What you do next is approximate the Borel function from its Taylor coefficients: Padé is the default, ConformalPade handles a cut rather than isolated poles, and aaa_borel (see Plotting for the extension) is the AAA alternative. All of them are AbstractBorelApproximants and plug into the same summation routines.","title":"Borel plane"},{"category":"section","location":"borel/#Padé-approximants","page":"Borel plane","text":"Padé over any field, with pole and residue extraction. borel_pade_poles locates the Borel singularities of a series - the practical route from coefficients to the actions A (instanton actions, in the physics vocabulary).","title":"Padé approximants"},{"category":"section","location":"borel/#Conformal-maps","page":"Borel plane","text":"For series whose Borel singularities form a cut rather than isolated poles, where Padé struggles.","title":"Conformal maps"},{"category":"type","location":"borel/#Resurgence.BorelSeries","page":"Borel plane","text":"BorelSeries{T} <: AbstractSeries\n\nThe Borel transform of a FormalSeries, as a distinct type so that a double Borel transform is a type error. Fields:\n\nseries::FormalSeries{T} - the ζ-plane series ζ^β-1 Σ b_n ζ^n with the reduced coefficients b_n = a_n  (β)_n (rising factorial (β)_n = β(β+1)(β+n-1)). The true Borel transform is 1/Γ(β) times this; that constant is applied inside laplace_sum.\nbeta::Rational{Int} - the leading ħ-power β of the source series.\nconstant_term::T - the ħ^0 coefficient of the source (a constant does not Borel-transform; it is split off here and re-added by summation).\nsource_var::Symbol - the variable of the source series (restored by inverse_borel).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.BorelSeries"},{"category":"method","location":"borel/#Resurgence.borel-Union{Tuple{FormalSeries{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Borel plane","text":"borel(Φ::FormalSeries; beta = power_offset(Φ), var = :ζ) -> BorelSeries\n\nExact Borel transform of Φ = ħ^β Σ a_n ħ^n:\n\nmathcalBΦ(ζ) = sum_n fraca_nΓ(n+β) ζ^n+β-1\n                   = fracζ^β-1Γ(β) sum_n fraca_n(β)_n ζ^n \n\nRequires beta ≥ 0. For beta == 0 the constant term a_0 is split off into the constant_term field and the rest is transformed with effective β = 1; the inverse and the Laplace sum restore it. The transform is exact in every coefficient type: inverse_borel(borel(Φ)) == Φ holds coefficient-by-coefficient.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.borel"},{"category":"method","location":"borel/#Resurgence.inverse_borel-Union{Tuple{BorelSeries{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Borel plane","text":"inverse_borel(B::BorelSeries) -> FormalSeries\n\nExact inverse of borel: multiplies the reduced coefficients back by the rising factorials and restores the source variable, power offset, and any split-off constant term. inverse_borel(borel(Φ)) == Φ in every coefficient type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.inverse_borel"},{"category":"type","location":"borel/#Resurgence.AbstractBorelApproximant","page":"Borel plane","text":"AbstractBorelApproximant\n\nAbstract supertype of approximants of the reduced Borel function Σ b_n ζ^n (the ζ^β-1Γ(β) factor stays with laplace_sum). The interface a subtype must provide:\n\ncallable at complex ζ: a(ζ) evaluates the approximant,\npoles(a; refine = true) returning the physical-sheet singularities as a Vector{Complex{BigFloat}} sorted by absolute value.\n\nAny such approximant plugs into the Laplace pipeline via laplace_sum(B, a, ħ) / lateral_sum(B, a, ħ). Subtypes: PadeApproximant, ConformalPade, and the AAAApproximant of the BaryRational package extension.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.AbstractBorelApproximant"},{"category":"type","location":"borel/#Resurgence.PadeApproximant","page":"Borel plane","text":"PadeApproximant{T}\n\nThe rational function LM(z) = p(z)q(z) with deg p = L, deg q = M, q(0) = 1, matching a power series through order z^L+M. Callable: r(z) evaluates p(z)q(z) by Horner. Fields p, q hold ascending coefficients; var names the variable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.PadeApproximant"},{"category":"method","location":"borel/#Resurgence.borel_pade_poles-Tuple{FormalSeries}","page":"Borel plane","text":"borel_pade_poles(Φ::FormalSeries; beta = power_offset(Φ), order = nothing,\n                 refine = true, reduce = false) -> Vector{Complex}\n\nThe one-liner: Borel transform, Padé approximation, pole extraction - the Padé estimate of the Borel-plane singularities of Φ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.borel_pade_poles"},{"category":"method","location":"borel/#Resurgence.pade-Tuple{BorelSeries}","page":"Borel plane","text":"pade(B::BorelSeries; order = nothing, reduce = false) -> PadeApproximant\n\nPadé approximant of the reduced Borel function Σ b_n ζ^n (the stored coefficients; the overall ζ^β-1Γ(β) factor does not move the poles and is re-applied by laplace_sum). With order = m builds the diagonal mm approximant (needs 2m+1 terms); by default uses all available terms, near-diagonally: M = N2, L = N - M for N = n_terms - 1. reduce = true degrades a degenerate request gracefully (see pade).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.pade"},{"category":"method","location":"borel/#Resurgence.pade-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractVector{T}, Integer, Integer}} where T","page":"Borel plane","text":"pade(c::AbstractVector, L, M; var = :ζ, reduce = false) -> PadeApproximant\npade(Φ::FormalSeries, L, M; reduce = false) -> PadeApproximant\n\nThe LM Padé approximant of the series with coefficients c (needs L + M + 1 of them; on a FormalSeries the polynomial part Σ a_n x^n is used, ignoring the power offset). Works over any field; throws DegeneratePade when the Toeplitz system is singular. With reduce = true a singular system is instead retried at L-1M-1 (walking down the degeneracy block, by Baker's block theorem, until the system is regular or M = 0) - the reduced approximant still matches the series through the originally requested order when the degeneracy is exact.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.pade"},{"category":"method","location":"borel/#Resurgence.poles-Tuple{PadeApproximant}","page":"Borel plane","text":"poles(r::PadeApproximant; refine = true) -> Vector{Complex}\n\nZeros of the denominator q: located via the companion-matrix eigenvalues in ComplexF64, then (with refine = true) polished by Newton iteration in Complex{BigFloat}. Sorted by absolute value - for a Borel–Padé approximant the smallest is the leading Borel singularity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.poles"},{"category":"method","location":"borel/#Resurgence.residues-Tuple{PadeApproximant}","page":"Borel plane","text":"residues(r::PadeApproximant; refine = true) -> Vector{Pair}\n\nPairs ζ₀ => p(ζ₀)/q′(ζ₀) for each simple pole of r, in the order of poles. This is the data alien derivatives consume (M2).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.residues"},{"category":"type","location":"borel/#Resurgence.ConformalMap","page":"Borel plane","text":"ConformalMap{T}\n\nThe conformal map of the ζ-plane cut along tζ_0  t  1 (and, with pair = true, also along -tζ_0  t  1) onto the unit w-disk, with 0  0 and the branch point(s) mapped to w = 1:\n\nsingle cut: w(ζ) = dfrac1 - sqrt1 - ζζ_01 + sqrt1 - ζζ_0, inverse ζ(w) = 4ζ_0 w(1+w)^2;\nsymmetric pair (ζ_0): w(ζ) = (1 - sqrt1 - ζ^2ζ_0^2)ζ_0ζ, inverse ζ(w) = 2ζ_0 w(1+w^2).\n\nNon-symmetric singularity pairs at arbitrary angles are out of scope - use the AAA extension or the multi-saddle large_order_fit in that regime. Callable: m(ζ) evaluates the forward map (in complex arithmetic); inverse maps back. Construct with conformal_map or via conformal_borel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.ConformalMap"},{"category":"type","location":"borel/#Resurgence.ConformalPade","page":"Borel plane","text":"ConformalPade{T} <: AbstractBorelApproximant\n\nA conformally mapped Borel approximant: the reduced Borel series re-expanded in the map variable w of a ConformalMap and approximated there by an inner PadeApproximant. Callable at ζ (c(ζ) = inner(w(ζ))); poles reports the branch point(s) plus the physical-sheet (w  1) inner poles pulled back to the ζ-plane. Construct with conformal_borel; plugs into laplace_sum through the approximant seam.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.ConformalPade"},{"category":"method","location":"borel/#Resurgence.conformal_borel-Tuple{BorelSeries}","page":"Borel plane","text":"conformal_borel(B::BorelSeries; zeta0 = nothing, pair = false, order = nothing,\n                inner_order = nothing) -> ConformalPade\n\nBuild the conformally mapped approximant of the reduced Borel function of B: place the branch point at zeta0 (default: the smallest-modulus Borel–Padé pole, computed with pade(B; order, reduce = true); throws NoSingularityFound when there is none), re-expand the series in the map variable w (exact for exact zeta0 and coefficients), and approximate in w: near-diagonally by default, inner_order = m for a diagonal mm, and inner_order = 0 for the pure re-expanded polynomial (already convergent on the disk). Sum with laplace_sum(B, conformal_borel(B), ħ).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.conformal_borel"},{"category":"method","location":"borel/#Resurgence.conformal_map-Tuple{Number}","page":"Borel plane","text":"conformal_map(zeta0; pair = false) -> ConformalMap\n\nThe ConformalMap placing the branch point at zeta0 (and, with pair = true, its mirror at -zeta0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.conformal_map"},{"category":"method","location":"borel/#Resurgence.inverse-Tuple{ConformalMap, Number}","page":"Borel plane","text":"inverse(m::ConformalMap, w) -> Number\n\nThe inverse map w  ζ: 4ζ_0 w(1+w)^2 (single cut) or 2ζ_0 w(1+w^2) (symmetric pair).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.inverse"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-index","page":"API index","text":"Everything exported by the package, alphabetically.\n\n","title":"API index"},{"category":"section","location":"transseries/#Transseries","page":"Transseries","text":"One-parameter Transseries sum_n sigma^n e^-nAhbarPhi_n, and MultiTransseries for several actions at once, stored sparsely over the action lattice.\n\nThe one- and many-action types share most of their interface - the accessors below dispatch on both.","title":"Transseries"},{"category":"section","location":"transseries/#Resonance","page":"Transseries","text":"When the actions satisfy integer relations the transseries develops logarithms. resonance_lattice computes the relations exactly over mathbbZ, and resonant_solve is the primitive that generates the log tower.","title":"Resonance"},{"category":"section","location":"transseries/#Solving-ODEs","page":"Transseries","text":"transseries_solve is a model-agnostic driver that builds a transseries solution sector by sector from a user-supplied linear solve.","title":"Solving ODEs"},{"category":"section","location":"transseries/#Painlevé-I","page":"Transseries","text":"painleve1 applies the driver to Painlevé I, with both normalization conventions of the instanton action available (:gikm by default, :string for the string-equation convention).","title":"Painlevé I"},{"category":"type","location":"transseries/#Resurgence.MultiTransseries","page":"Transseries","text":"MultiTransseries{T,A,K,S} <: AbstractSeries\n\nA K-parameter transseries F(σ ħ) = sum_n  ℤ_0^K Big(prod_i σ_i^n_iBig) e^-(nA)ħ Φ_n(ħ) with action lattice actions::NTuple{K,A} and sectors sectors::Dict{NTuple{K,Int},S} (multi-index n ↦ Φ_n). Absent keys inside the componentwise bounding box of the stored support are exact zeros; keys outside it are unknown (truncated). The parameter vector σ is not stored - it is supplied to transseries_sum, and the Stokes automorphism acts on it (see stokes_automorphism).\n\nThe sector type S is FormalSeries{T} for the ordinary non-resonant case and LogSeries{T} when any sector carries log ħ blocks - which resonance forces, see resonant_solve. It is homogeneous (a log-free sector inside a resonant transseries is a one-block LogSeries), so the Dict stays type-stable, and it is inferred from the sectors you pass; MultiTransseries{T,A,K} remains a valid type to dispatch on.\n\nK = 1 embeds the one-parameter Transseries: MultiTransseries(F) and Transseries(mt) are inverse (a mandatory round-trip).\n\nExamples\n\nmt = MultiTransseries((1//1, 2//1), Dict((0,0) => Φ00, (1,0) => Φ10))\nsector(mt, (1, 0))                                # Φ_{(1,0)}\nweight(mt, (1, 1))                                # n·A = 1·1 + 1·2 = 3\nMultiTransseries(Transseries(:euler, 12))         # rank-1 embedding\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.MultiTransseries"},{"category":"method","location":"transseries/#Resurgence.MultiTransseries-Union{Tuple{Transseries{T, A}}, Tuple{A}, Tuple{T}} where {T, A}","page":"Transseries","text":"MultiTransseries(F::Transseries) -> MultiTransseries{T,A,1}\n\nEmbed a one-parameter Transseries as the rank-1 case (keys (n,)). Inverse of Transseries(::MultiTransseries); the round-trip is exact.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.MultiTransseries"},{"category":"type","location":"transseries/#Resurgence.Transseries","page":"Transseries","text":"Transseries{T,A} <: AbstractSeries\n\nA one-parameter transseries F(σ ħ) = sum_n0 σ^n e^-nAħ Φ_n(ħ) with action action::A and sectors sectors::Vector{FormalSeries{T}} (dense in the integer exponential weight: sectors[k] is Φ_k-1). The prefactor ħ^β_n of each sector lives in its power_offset. The transseries parameter σ is not stored; it is supplied to transseries_sum, and the Stokes automorphism acts on it (see stokes_automorphism).\n\nExamples\n\nF = Transseries(:euler, 20)                       # named course-canon transseries\nF = Transseries(1//1, [Φ₀, Φ₁])                   # from explicit sectors\nsector(F, 1)                                      # Φ₁\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.Transseries"},{"category":"method","location":"transseries/#Resurgence.Transseries-Union{Tuple{MultiTransseries{T, A, K}}, Tuple{K}, Tuple{A}, Tuple{T}} where {T, A, K}","page":"Transseries","text":"Transseries(mt::MultiTransseries{T,A,1}) -> Transseries\n\nProject a rank-1 MultiTransseries back to a one-parameter Transseries (inverse of the embedding). Throws InvalidArgument for K ≠ 1, and IncompatibleSeries when a sector carries genuine log ħ blocks (the one-parameter Transseries has no representation for those).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.Transseries"},{"category":"method","location":"transseries/#Base.:*-Union{Tuple{S2}, Tuple{K2}, Tuple{A2}, Tuple{T2}, Tuple{S1}, Tuple{K1}, Tuple{A1}, Tuple{T1}, Tuple{MultiTransseries{T1, A1, K1, S1}, MultiTransseries{T2, A2, K2, S2}}} where {T1, A1, K1, S1, T2, A2, K2, S2}","page":"Transseries","text":"F * G\n\nWeight-graded Cauchy product (FG)_n = sum_i+j=n Φ_i Ψ_j (the exponential weights add: e^-(iA)ħ e^-(jA)ħ = e^-(nA)ħ), truncated to the componentwise min of the two operands' bounding boxes - the frontier where every contribution is available. Reduces to the one-parameter min(n_sectors,…) at K = 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.:*"},{"category":"method","location":"transseries/#Base.:*-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Transseries{T}, Transseries{T}}} where T","page":"Transseries","text":"F * G\n\nWeight-graded Cauchy product (FG)_n = sum_i=0^n Φ_i Ψ_n-i (the exponential weights multiply as e^-iAħ e^-(n-i)Aħ = e^-nAħ), truncated to min(n_sectors(F), n_sectors(G)) sectors - the highest weight at which every contribution is available.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.:*"},{"category":"method","location":"transseries/#Base.truncate-Tuple{Transseries}","page":"Transseries","text":"truncate(F::Transseries; sectors = n_sectors(F), terms = nothing) -> Transseries\n\nThe transseries truncated to its first sectors sectors, each truncated to terms coefficients (nothing keeps every sector's own length).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.truncate"},{"category":"method","location":"transseries/#Base.truncate-Union{Tuple{MultiTransseries{T, A, K, S}}, Tuple{S}, Tuple{K}, Tuple{A}, Tuple{T}} where {T, A, K, S}","page":"Transseries","text":"truncate(mt::MultiTransseries; degree = nothing, terms = nothing) -> MultiTransseries\n\nThe transseries truncated to the sectors with multi-index ≼ degree componentwise (degree an Integer, applied to every direction, or an NTuple; nothing keeps all), each sector truncated to terms coefficients (nothing keeps every sector's length).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.truncate"},{"category":"method","location":"transseries/#Resurgence.action-Tuple{Transseries}","page":"Transseries","text":"action(F::Transseries) -> Number\n\nThe action A of the exponential weights e^-nAħ. Its sign convention: A is the location of the leading Borel-plane singularity of Φ_0 (so the weights e^-nAħ are exponentially small in the directions where the perturbative sector dominates).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.action"},{"category":"method","location":"transseries/#Resurgence.actions-Tuple{MultiTransseries}","page":"Transseries","text":"actions(mt::MultiTransseries) -> NTuple{K}\n\nThe action lattice A = (A_1A_K) of the exponential weights e^-(nA)ħ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.actions"},{"category":"method","location":"transseries/#Resurgence.is_resonant-Tuple{MultiTransseries}","page":"Transseries","text":"is_resonant(mt::MultiTransseries) -> Bool\n\ntrue when two distinct stored sectors share the same transmonomial weight nA - the support-level resonance condition (distinct instanton numbers meeting on one ray).\n\nThis asks only about the sectors actually stored. The stronger, structural question - can any pair of sectors collide, i.e. does the weight map have a nonzero kernel - is is_resonant(::Tuple) on the actions, whose kernel is resonance_lattice.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.is_resonant"},{"category":"method","location":"transseries/#Resurgence.log_degree-Tuple{MultiTransseries}","page":"Transseries","text":"log_degree(mt::MultiTransseries[, n]) -> Int\n\nThe log ħ degree of the sector at multi-index n, or the maximum over all stored sectors when n is omitted. Always 0 for a non-resonant transseries (sector type FormalSeries). Bounded by resonance_depth.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.log_degree"},{"category":"method","location":"transseries/#Resurgence.n_actions-Union{Tuple{MultiTransseries{T, A, K}}, Tuple{K}, Tuple{A}, Tuple{T}} where {T, A, K}","page":"Transseries","text":"n_actions(mt::MultiTransseries) -> Int\n\nThe lattice rank K (number of independent instanton actions / parameters).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.n_actions"},{"category":"method","location":"transseries/#Resurgence.n_sectors-Tuple{MultiTransseries}","page":"Transseries","text":"n_sectors(mt::MultiTransseries) -> Int\n\nNumber of stored (nonzero) sectors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.n_sectors"},{"category":"method","location":"transseries/#Resurgence.n_sectors-Tuple{Transseries}","page":"Transseries","text":"n_sectors(F::Transseries) -> Int\n\nNumber of stored sectors (the highest stored exponential weight is n_sectors(F) - 1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.n_sectors"},{"category":"method","location":"transseries/#Resurgence.sector-Tuple{Transseries, Integer}","page":"Transseries","text":"sector(F::Transseries, n::Integer) -> FormalSeries\n\nThe sector Φ_n of weight e^-nAħ (0-based; n ≥ n_sectors(F) returns the zero series, mirroring Φ[n] on FormalSeries).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.sector"},{"category":"method","location":"transseries/#Resurgence.sector-Union{Tuple{N}, Tuple{K}, Tuple{A}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{MultiTransseries{T, A, K}, NTuple{N, Integer}}} where {T, A, K, N}","page":"Transseries","text":"sector(mt::MultiTransseries, n) -> FormalSeries or LogSeries\n\nThe sector Φ_n at multi-index n (an NTuple or varargs of nonnegative integers); an absent key returns the zero series, mirroring Φ[n] on FormalSeries. The returned type is the transseries' sector type S (see MultiTransseries).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.sector"},{"category":"method","location":"transseries/#Resurgence.sector_indices-Tuple{MultiTransseries}","page":"Transseries","text":"sector_indices(mt::MultiTransseries) -> Vector{NTuple{K,Int}}\n\nThe stored multi-indices, sorted lexicographically (deterministic iteration order).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.sector_indices"},{"category":"method","location":"transseries/#Resurgence.sectors-Tuple{MultiTransseries}","page":"Transseries","text":"sectors(mt::MultiTransseries) -> Dict\n\nThe sector map n  Φ_n (a copy; transseries are immutable).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.sectors"},{"category":"method","location":"transseries/#Resurgence.sectors-Tuple{Transseries}","page":"Transseries","text":"sectors(F::Transseries) -> Vector{FormalSeries}\n\nThe sector list Φ_0 Φ_1  (a copy; transseries are immutable).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.sectors"},{"category":"method","location":"transseries/#Resurgence.transseries_sum-Tuple{Transseries, Number, Number}","page":"Transseries","text":"transseries_sum(F::Transseries, σ, ħ; θ = 0, side = :plus, tilt = 1//100,\n                order = nothing, rtol = eps^(3/4)) -> Complex\n\nLateral Borel sum of the transseries at parameter σ: sum_n σ^n e^-nAħ s_θ^(Φ_n)(ħ), where each sector is summed by lateral_sum of its Borel transform along the ray θ (constant sectors, e.g. a pure one-instanton term Φ_1 = 1, are evaluated exactly instead).\n\nThe two lateral sums are related by the Stokes automorphism acting on the parameter: transseries_sum(F, σ, ħ; side = :minus) equals transseries_sum(F, σ + S₁, ħ; side = :plus) - see stokes_automorphism.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.transseries_sum"},{"category":"method","location":"transseries/#Resurgence.transseries_sum-Union{Tuple{K}, Tuple{A}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{MultiTransseries{T, A, K}, Tuple, Number}} where {T, A, K}","page":"Transseries","text":"transseries_sum(F::MultiTransseries, σ, ħ; θ = 0, side = :plus, log_ħ = log(complex(ħ)),\n                kwargs...) -> Complex\n\nLateral Borel sum of the K-parameter transseries at parameter vector σ (an NTuple of length K; a scalar is accepted for K = 1): sum_n big(prod_i σ_i^n_ibig) e^-(nA)ħ s_θ^(Φ_n)(ħ). Each non-constant sector is summed by lateral_sum of its Borel transform along the ray θ; constant sectors are evaluated exactly. Several Borel singularities per ray are handled sector by sector. See stokes_automorphism for the side↔parameter-shift relation.\n\nResonant sectors carry log ħ blocks (LogSeries). log ħ is inert under Borel–Laplace - it does not depend on the Borel variable ζ - so each block is summed independently and weighted by log_ħ^p. log_ħ defaults to the principal branch, which is correct for θ ∈ (-π, π]; a transseries continued past that (across several Stokes rays) lives on the universal cover of ħ = 0, and you pass the continued value explicitly. The monodromy log_ħ ↦ log_ħ + 2πi genuinely changes the sum - that is resonance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.transseries_sum"},{"category":"method","location":"transseries/#Resurgence.weight-Union{Tuple{K}, Tuple{A}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{MultiTransseries{T, A, K}, NTuple{K, Integer}}} where {T, A, K}","page":"Transseries","text":"weight(mt::MultiTransseries, n) -> Number\n\nThe transmonomial exponent nA = sum_i n_i A_i of the sector at multi-index n.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.weight"},{"category":"method","location":"transseries/#Resurgence.charges-Union{Tuple{K}, Tuple{A}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{MultiTransseries{T, A, K}, Number}} where {T, A, K}","page":"Transseries","text":"charges(mt::MultiTransseries, ω) -> Vector{NTuple{K,Int}}\n\nThe fiber over a singularity location ω: every nonzero charge ℓ ≽ 0 inside the bounding box of mt's support with ℓ·A = ω, sorted lexicographically. At a non-resonant lattice this is a single charge (or empty); at a resonant one it is the whole tower - for A = (A, -A) the fiber over ω = A is (1,0), (2,1), (3,2), …, which is precisely why a resonant Δ_ω carries independent Stokes data per charge (see alien_derivative).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.charges"},{"category":"method","location":"transseries/#Resurgence.is_resonant-Tuple{Tuple}","page":"Transseries","text":"is_resonant(actions::Tuple; lattice = nothing) -> Bool\n\ntrue when the action lattice itself is resonant - the weight map k ↦ k·A has a nonzero integer kernel (see resonance_lattice), so some pair of distinct sectors must share a transmonomial and logs are possible.\n\nThis is the lattice-level predicate. The is_resonant(::MultiTransseries) method is the weaker support-level one: it asks whether two sectors that are actually stored collide.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.is_resonant"},{"category":"method","location":"transseries/#Resurgence.resonance_depth-Union{Tuple{K}, Tuple{A}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{MultiTransseries{T, A, K}, NTuple{K, Integer}}} where {T, A, K}","page":"Transseries","text":"resonance_depth(mt::MultiTransseries, n; lattice = nothing) -> Int\n\nHow many times the sector n can be walked back along the resonance lattice and stay in ℤ_{≥0}^K: maxm  n - mk  0 for the ≽ 0 generator k of resonance_lattice. Since each resonant inversion adds one power of log, this bounds the log degree of Φ_n, and the bound is attained (see resonant_solve).\n\nFor A = (A, -A) with k = (1,1) this is min(n₁, n₂). Returns 0 for a non-resonant lattice. Throws when the lattice has no ≽ 0 generator - e.g. A = (1, 2) (kernel (2,-1)) or duplicated actions A = (a, a) (kernel (1,-1)): sectors collide there, but no positive walk generates them, and the correct depth is model-dependent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.resonance_depth"},{"category":"method","location":"transseries/#Resurgence.resonance_lattice-Tuple{Tuple}","page":"Transseries","text":"resonance_lattice(actions; lattice = nothing) -> Vector{NTuple{K,Int}}\nresonance_lattice(mt::MultiTransseries) -> Vector{NTuple{K,Int}}\n\nA ℤ-basis of the resonance lattice Λ = k  ℤ^K  kA = 0 - the kernel of the weight map. Λ ≠ 0 is exactly the condition for distinct sectors to share a transmonomial, i.e. for is_resonant to hold and for logs to appear; Λ = 0 (always the case at K = 1, since A_1 ≠ 0) means the transseries is log-free.\n\nEach basis vector is sign-canonicalized (first nonzero entry positive). Note that a kernel vector need not be ≽ 0: A = (1, 2) has Λ = ℤ·(2,-1), so sectors (2,0) and (0,1) collide even though no positive combination vanishes. resonance_depth needs a ≽ 0 generator and says so when there is none.\n\nExact actions only. Rational (or complex-rational) actions give the kernel exactly; inexact ones - Painlevé I's A = 8√6/5 among them - throw, since deciding a ℚ-linear relation between floats is not a well-posed question. Pass the kernel yourself via lattice in that case (every consumer here takes the same keyword).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.resonance_lattice"},{"category":"method","location":"transseries/#Resurgence.resonant_solve-Tuple{FormalSeries, Number}","page":"Transseries","text":"resonant_solve(g, detuning) -> LogSeries\n\nSolve the transseries channel equation (ħ^2 _ħ + c)u = g for u, where g is a FormalSeries or LogSeries and c = detuning. This is the primitive that creates log sectors - see the discussion at the top of resonance.jl.\n\ndetuning ≠ 0: the solution is unique and the log degree is preserved (log_degree(u) == log_degree(g)); with g log-free, so is u.\ndetuning == 0 (resonance): the log degree grows by one, with the top block known in closed form as u^P+1 = (ħ^1 g^P)(P+1). The remaining integration constant (the ħ^0 coefficient of u^{[0]}, which ħ^2 ∂_ħ annihilates) is fixed to zero.\n\nAt detuning == 0 the growth is conditional on the source actually hitting the cokernel: if power_offset(g) ∉ ℤ the power grid misses ħ^1 entirely and u stays log-free.\n\nExamples\n\nresonant_solve(FormalSeries([0//1, 1//1]), 0//1)   # (ħ²∂)u = ħ  ⇒  u = log ħ\nresonant_solve(FormalSeries([1//1]), 2//1)         # detuned  ⇒  log-free\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.resonant_solve"},{"category":"method","location":"transseries/#Resurgence._solve_linear_sector-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Any, FormalSeries{T}, Rational{Int64}, Integer, Integer}} where T","page":"Transseries","text":"_solve_linear_sector(apply_L, g, β, δ, N; free = Int[], freeval = 1) -> FormalSeries\n\nOrder-by-order solution of a linear sector equation apply_L(Φ) = g for the fluctuation Φ = Σ_m b_m s^{β+m} on the unit grid (length N, leading power β). apply_L is the sector's linearized operator (a FormalSeries -> FormalSeries closure). δ is the operator's diagonal shift - apply_L sends a coefficient at power p to a leading output at p + δ - so the coefficient at grid index m is fixed by the residual at power β + m + δ. Grid positions whose probed diagonal vanishes are skipped (this absorbs any step > 1 sparsity automatically), and indices in free are integration constants: set to freeval and never solved (the homogeneous mode carrying the transseries parameter).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence._solve_linear_sector"},{"category":"method","location":"transseries/#Resurgence.transseries_solve-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{FormalSeries{T}, Number, Any}} where T","page":"Transseries","text":"transseries_solve(seed, action, solve_sector; sectors = 2, var = variable(seed))\n    -> Transseries\n\nAssemble a one-parameter transseries u = sum_k0 C^k e^-kAħ Φ_k from its perturbative seed Φ_0 = seed (a FormalSeries) by the sector recursion: for each k = 1, …, sectors-1, Φ_k = solve_sector(Φ₀…Φ_{k-1}, k), where solve_sector is a closure (sofar::Vector{FormalSeries}, k::Int) -> FormalSeries that performs the model's per-sector linear (Newton) solve. The result is a Transseries with the given action.\n\nThis is the model-agnostic driver; the per-sector linear solve - the ODE's linearized operator and the quadratic source assembled from sofar - is the caller's closure. See painleve1 for the Painlevé I instance and _solve_linear_sector for the order-by-order primitive most closures build on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.transseries_solve"},{"category":"method","location":"transseries/#Resurgence._painleve1_residual-Union{Tuple{FormalSeries{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Transseries","text":"_painleve1_residual(u0; convention = :gikm) -> FormalSeries\n\nThe Painlevé I residual −c(s^{10}u₀″ + 5 s⁹ u₀′)/16 + u₀² − s^{-4} on the s-grid, which vanishes to truncation order for the true perturbative u₀ (the \"every theorem is a test\" oracle for FormalSeries(:painleve1, n)). Exact in the coefficient type of u0. Here c = 1/6 (:gikm) or 1/12 (:string) and ∂_z² = (s^{10}∂_s² + 5s⁹∂_s)/16.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence._painleve1_residual"},{"category":"method","location":"transseries/#Resurgence.painleve1-Tuple{Integer}","page":"Transseries","text":"painleve1(n; sectors = 2, convention = :gikm, T = BigFloat) -> Transseries\n\nThe Painlevé I transseries solution, sectors solved by the transseries_solve recursion in the variable s = z^{-1/4}: sector 0 is the perturbative u₀ and sector k ≥ 1 is the one-Newton-step fluctuation of the transmonomial e^{-kA s^{-5}} (= e^{-kA z^{5/4}}). Each sector carries n fluctuation orders. The action is A = painleve1_action(; convention); the coefficient type T defaults to BigFloat.\n\nThe returned Transseries is in the s variable (so its action multiplies s^{-5} = z^{5/4}), with sector 0 the exact perturbative series cast to T. The resonant two-parameter (A, −A) structure - where s-independent logs appear on the diagonal - is built with lattice = [(1, 1)]; see the tests and resonance_lattice.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.painleve1"},{"category":"method","location":"transseries/#Resurgence.painleve1_action-Tuple{}","page":"Transseries","text":"painleve1_action(; convention = :gikm, T = BigFloat) -> T\n\nThe instanton action A = 8√3/5 ≈ 2.7713 (:gikm) or A = 8√6/5 ≈ 3.9192 (:string), as a T-valued float. A is inexact, so a resonant Painlevé I transseries must be handed the resonance kernel explicitly - lattice = [(1, 1)] for the (A, −A) lattice (see resonance_lattice). The exact square is painleve1_action_squared.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.painleve1_action"},{"category":"method","location":"transseries/#Resurgence.painleve1_action_squared-Tuple{}","page":"Transseries","text":"painleve1_action_squared(; convention = :gikm) -> Rational{Int}\n\nThe exact squared instanton action A² of Painlevé I: 192//25 in the GIKM normalization −(1/6)u″ + u² = z, 384//25 in the :string normalization −(1/12)u″ + u² = z. Rational even though A itself is irrational - this is the exact oracle for painleve1_action. Derived from the leading balance A = (4/5)√(2/c) for −c·u″ + u² = z.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.painleve1_action_squared"},{"category":"section","location":"large_order/#Large-order-analysis","page":"Large-order analysis","text":"large_order_fit recovers (A b S) from a_n sim SGamma(n+b)A^n, including multi-saddle fits with complex-conjugate action pairs.","title":"Large-order analysis"},{"category":"section","location":"large_order/#Peeling-off-a-known-singularity","page":"Large-order analysis","text":"subtract_singularity removes a known Borel singularity to expose the subleading one.","title":"Peeling off a known singularity"},{"category":"method","location":"large_order/#Resurgence.large_order_fit-Tuple{FormalSeries}","page":"Large-order analysis","text":"large_order_fit(Φ::FormalSeries; order = 4, method = :richardson)\n    -> NamedTuple{(:A, :b, :S)}\nlarge_order_fit(Φ::FormalSeries; saddles = J, order = nothing,\n                window = nothing, maxiter = 50)\n    -> NamedTuple{(:saddles, :converged)}\n\nFit the factorial growth a_n sim S Γ(p_n + b) A^-p_n of the coefficients, where p_n = n + mathrmpower_offset is the actual ħ-power of each term. Returns (A = …, b = …, S = …); A is the modulus of the action - the distance of the leading Borel-plane singularity - extracted by Richardson-extrapolating (order sweeps) the ratio sequences\n\nρ_n+1 - ρ_n  1A with ρ_n = a_n+1a_n,\nA ρ_n - p_n  b,\na_n A^p_n  Γ(p_n + b)  S.\n\nOracles: the Euler series gives (1 0 1) exactly; the Airy series gives A = 43. Precision follows the coefficient type (Rational{BigInt} coefficients are fitted in BigFloat). method selects the extrapolation used on the three sequences - any accelerate method (order is passed through as its depth).\n\nWith saddles = J > 1 the multi-saddle model a_n  sum_j=1^J S_j Γ(p_n + b_j) A_j^-p_n is fitted instead (complex actions A_j - the Borel singularity positions themselves, which also initialize the fit; real b_j): amplitudes by linear least squares over the fit window (0-based coefficient indices; default the last half), actions and exponents by damped Gauss–Newton (maxiter iterations; here order is passed to the initializing pade). For real coefficients, complex-conjugate action pairs are detected and fitted as one oscillatory group 2ReSΓ(p_n+b)A^-p_n - the equal-modulus A bar A case where the single-saddle ratio fit fails. Returns (saddles = [(A, b, S), …] sorted by |A|, converged::Bool) without throwing on non-convergence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.large_order_fit"},{"category":"method","location":"large_order/#Resurgence.richardson-Tuple{AbstractVector, Integer}","page":"Large-order analysis","text":"richardson(seq::AbstractVector, order::Integer) -> Number\n\nRichardson extrapolation of a sequence s_n = s_ + c_1n + c_2n^2 +  (the element seq[n] is taken as s_n): performs order elimination sweeps s_n  ((n+k)s_n+1 - ns_n)k and returns the last surviving element, accurate to O(n^-(order+1)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.richardson"},{"category":"method","location":"large_order/#Resurgence.subtract_singularity-Tuple{BorelSeries, Number}","page":"Large-order analysis","text":"subtract_singularity(B::BorelSeries, zeta0; beta = 1, c = nothing,\n                     order = nothing, method = :wynn, accel_order = nothing)\n    -> NamedTuple{(:peeled, :c, :zeta0, :beta)}\nsubtract_singularity(B::BorelSeries; kwargs...)\n\nDarboux peeling: model the dominant singularity of the reduced Borel function as c(1 - ζζ_0)^-β (Taylor coefficients s_n = c(β)_n ζ_0^-nn), subtract it coefficient-by-coefficient, and return the peeled BorelSeries (same B.beta, constant term, and source variable) - so poles(pade(out.peeled)) or large_order_fit(inverse_borel(out.peeled)) can resolve the subleading singularity/action that the dominant one masked.\n\nHere beta is the singularity exponent β - not the B.beta of the Borel normalization; beta = 1 is a simple pole. The amplitude c is estimated when not given: for beta = 1 from the Padé residue nearest zeta0 (c = -mathrmresζ_0, exact for a rational Borel function); for general beta by accelerateing the sequence b_n ζ_0^n n(β)_n  c (kwargs method, accel_order). Without an explicit zeta0 the smallest-modulus Borel–Padé pole is used (order is passed to pade); throws NoSingularityFound when there is none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.subtract_singularity"},{"category":"section","location":"series/#Series","page":"Series","text":"FormalSeries is a truncated power series over any coefficient type, with arithmetic, truncation, differentiation and fractional power offsets. LogSeries adds a polynomial in loghbar, which is what resonant problems produce.\n\nFormalSeries(:name, n) builds a named example from its exact recursion. These double as test oracles - the suite checks mathematical identities rather than stored numbers.","title":"Series"},{"category":"section","location":"series/#Series-with-logarithms","page":"Series","text":"","title":"Series with logarithms"},{"category":"section","location":"series/#Named-examples","page":"Series","text":"","title":"Named examples"},{"category":"type","location":"series/#Resurgence.AbstractSeries","page":"Series","text":"AbstractSeries\n\nAbstract supertype of all series-like objects in Resurgence.jl (FormalSeries, BorelSeries).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.AbstractSeries"},{"category":"type","location":"series/#Resurgence.FormalSeries","page":"Series","text":"FormalSeries{T} <: AbstractSeries\n\nA truncated formal power series x^β_0 sum_n=0^N a_n x^n in the variable var (default :ħ), with coefficients coeffs::Vector{T} (so coeffs[k] is a_k-1) and rational leading power power_offset = β_0.\n\nThe coefficient type T is fully generic: exact rationals, BigFloats, complex numbers, and ring elements from computer-algebra systems all work.\n\nExamples\n\nΦ = FormalSeries([1//1, 1, 2, 6], :ħ)            # Σ n! ħ^n, 4 terms\nΦ = FormalSeries([1.0, 1.0], :x; power_offset = 1//2)   # √x (1 + x)\nΦ[2]                                              # coefficient a₂ == 2\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.FormalSeries"},{"category":"method","location":"series/#Base.:*-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{FormalSeries{T}, FormalSeries{T}}} where T","page":"Series","text":"Φ * Ψ\n\nCauchy product, truncated to min(n_terms(Φ), n_terms(Ψ)) terms; power offsets add.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.:*"},{"category":"method","location":"series/#Base.getindex-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{FormalSeries{T}, Integer}} where T","page":"Series","text":"Φ[n]\n\nCoefficient a_n of x^β_0 + n (0-based; n ≥ n_terms(Φ) returns zero).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.getindex"},{"category":"method","location":"series/#Base.truncate-Tuple{FormalSeries, Integer}","page":"Series","text":"truncate(Φ::FormalSeries, n) -> FormalSeries\n\nThe series truncated to its first n coefficients.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.truncate"},{"category":"method","location":"series/#Resurgence.coefficients-Tuple{FormalSeries}","page":"Series","text":"coefficients(Φ::AbstractSeries) -> Vector\n\nThe coefficient vector a_0 a_1  (a copy; series are immutable).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.coefficients"},{"category":"method","location":"series/#Resurgence.derivative-Tuple{FormalSeries}","page":"Series","text":"derivative(Φ::FormalSeries) -> FormalSeries\n\nThe termwise derivative _x: x^β_0 sum a_n x^n mapsto x^β_0 - 1 sum (β_0 + n) a_n x^n. Exact in the coefficient type whenever the powers are integral. The companion operator x^2 _x - the one that governs transseries sector equations, see resonant_solve - is _shift_power(derivative(Φ), 2).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.derivative"},{"category":"method","location":"series/#Resurgence.evaluate-Tuple{FormalSeries, Any}","page":"Series","text":"evaluate(Φ::FormalSeries, x) -> Number\n\nEvaluate the truncated series at x (Horner on the polynomial part, times x^power_offset). This is formal evaluation of the truncation - for the resummed value of a divergent series use borel_sum.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.evaluate"},{"category":"method","location":"series/#Resurgence.is_exact-Tuple{FormalSeries}","page":"Series","text":"is_exact(Φ::AbstractSeries) -> Bool\n\ntrue when the coefficient type supports exact arithmetic (integers, rationals, and non-AbstractFloat number types such as computer-algebra ring elements).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.is_exact"},{"category":"method","location":"series/#Resurgence.n_terms-Tuple{FormalSeries}","page":"Series","text":"n_terms(Φ::AbstractSeries) -> Int\n\nNumber of stored coefficients (the truncation order is n_terms(Φ) - 1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.n_terms"},{"category":"method","location":"series/#Resurgence.power_offset-Tuple{FormalSeries}","page":"Series","text":"power_offset(Φ::FormalSeries) -> Rational{Int}\n\nThe rational leading power β_0 in x^β_0 Σ a_n x^n.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.power_offset"},{"category":"method","location":"series/#Resurgence.variable-Tuple{FormalSeries}","page":"Series","text":"variable(Φ::AbstractSeries) -> Symbol\n\nThe variable the series is written in.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.variable"},{"category":"method","location":"series/#Resurgence.FormalSeries-Tuple{LogSeries}","page":"Series","text":"FormalSeries(L::LogSeries) -> FormalSeries\n\nProject a log-free LogSeries back to a FormalSeries (inverse of the embedding). Throws IncompatibleSeries when log_degree(L) > 0 - a genuine log block has no FormalSeries representation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.FormalSeries"},{"category":"type","location":"series/#Resurgence.LogSeries","page":"Series","text":"LogSeries{T} <: AbstractSeries\n\nA series polynomial in log(x) with FormalSeries coefficients: Φ(x) = sum_p=0^P log(x)^p Φ^p(x), stored as blocks::Vector{FormalSeries{T}} with blocks[p+1] = Φ^p. Trailing zero blocks are dropped, so log_degree = P is the true degree; a log-free LogSeries has one block and FormalSeries(L) projects it back (the round-trip with LogSeries(::FormalSeries) is exact).\n\nBlocks each carry their own power_offset - resonant_solve generally produces blocks on different power grids.\n\nConvention: the basis is log(x) itself, not log(1/x) or log(x/A). A rescaling log(c·x^r) is a unipotent triangular change of basis in the log degree, hence pure convention; this one is fixed so that x^2 _x log x = x.\n\nExamples\n\nL = LogSeries([FormalSeries([0//1]), FormalSeries([1//1])])   # log(ħ)\nlog_degree(L)                                                  # 1\nlog_block(L, 1)                                                # the coefficient of log(ħ)\nevaluate(L, 0.5)                                               # log(0.5) + 0.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.LogSeries"},{"category":"method","location":"series/#Resurgence.LogSeries-Union{Tuple{FormalSeries{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Series","text":"LogSeries(Φ::FormalSeries) -> LogSeries\n\nEmbed a FormalSeries as the log-degree-0 case. Inverse of FormalSeries(::LogSeries).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.LogSeries"},{"category":"method","location":"series/#Base.:*-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{LogSeries{T}, LogSeries{T}}} where T","page":"Series","text":"L * M\n\nProduct of two log-block polynomials: Cauchy in the log degree (degrees add - a log block is exact, unlike the truncated x direction) with each block product the truncated Cauchy product of FormalSeries.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.:*"},{"category":"method","location":"series/#Base.truncate-Tuple{LogSeries}","page":"Series","text":"truncate(L::LogSeries; degree = nothing, terms = nothing) -> LogSeries\n\nTruncate to log degree ≤ degree (nothing keeps every block) with each block cut to terms coefficients (nothing keeps each block's length).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.truncate"},{"category":"method","location":"series/#Resurgence.derivative-Tuple{LogSeries}","page":"Series","text":"derivative(L::LogSeries) -> LogSeries\n\nThe derivative _x, by Leibniz on each block: _x(log^p x  Φ) = log^p x  Φ + p log^p-1 x  x^-1Φ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.derivative"},{"category":"method","location":"series/#Resurgence.evaluate-Tuple{LogSeries, Any}","page":"Series","text":"evaluate(L::LogSeries, x; log_x = log(complex(x))) -> Number\n\nEvaluate the truncated log-block polynomial at x, formally in each block (see evaluate(::FormalSeries, x)).\n\nlog_x defaults to the principal branch. A transseries lives on a sector of the universal cover of x = 0, so a continued arg x may leave (-π, π]; pass the continued value explicitly in that case. The monodromy log_x ↦ log_x + 2πi mixes the blocks - that is physical, not an artifact.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.evaluate"},{"category":"method","location":"series/#Resurgence.log_block-Tuple{LogSeries, Integer}","page":"Series","text":"log_block(L::LogSeries, p) -> FormalSeries\n\nThe block Φ^p multiplying log(x)^p (0-based); p > log_degree(L) returns the zero series, mirroring Φ[n] on FormalSeries.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.log_block"},{"category":"method","location":"series/#Resurgence.log_blocks-Tuple{LogSeries}","page":"Series","text":"log_blocks(L::LogSeries) -> Vector{FormalSeries}\n\nThe blocks Φ^0 Φ^1  (a copy; series are immutable).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.log_blocks"},{"category":"method","location":"series/#Resurgence.log_degree-Tuple{LogSeries}","page":"Series","text":"log_degree(L::LogSeries) -> Int\n\nThe degree P in log(x) (trailing zero blocks are dropped, so this is exact).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.log_degree"},{"category":"method","location":"series/#Resurgence.FormalSeries-Tuple{Symbol, Integer}","page":"Series","text":"FormalSeries(name::Symbol, n::Integer; var = :ħ) -> FormalSeries{Rational{BigInt}}\n\nNamed course-canon series with n exact rational coefficients:\n\n:euler - the Euler-equation series sum_k0 k(-ħ)^k+1 = -ħ + ħ^2 - 2ħ^3 +  (power offset 1); its Borel transform is exactly -1(1+ζ).\n:airy - the Airy asymptotic series in the WKB variable ħ = z^-32: sum_k0 (-1)^k u_k (tfrac32 ħ)^k with u_0 = 1, u_k+1 = u_k (6k+5)(6k+1)  (72(k+1)), the series Φ(ħ) in mathrmAi(z)  e^-ξ Φ(ħ)  (2sqrtπ z^14), ξ = tfrac23 z^32 = tfrac23ħ. Its Borel singularity sits at ζ = -43, the Airy action.\n:airy_bi - the second Airy sector sum_k0 u_k (tfrac32 ħ)^k (the same u_k without the alternating sign), the series Ψ(ħ) in mathrmBi(z)  e^ξ Ψ(ħ)  (sqrtπ z^14); Borel singularity at ζ = +43. This is the one-instanton sector of the Airy transseries: Δ_A Φ = S_1 Ψ (see alien_derivative).\n:quartic - the Bender–Wu perturbative ground-state energy of the quartic anharmonic oscillator H = p^22 + x^22 + gx^4: E_0(g) = tfrac12 + tfrac34 g - tfrac218 g^2 + tfrac33316 g^3 -  (exact rationals from the wavefunction recursion; large order E_n sim -(sqrt6π^32)(-3)^nΓ(n+tfrac12), instanton action 1/3).\n:phi4 - the zero-dimensional φ⁴ partition function Z(g) = tfrac1sqrt2π _-^ e^-x^22 - g x^4dx = sum_n0 tfrac(-1)^n (4n-1)n g^n; the n-th coefficient counts the Wick contractions (vacuum Feynman diagrams) at order n. Closed form a_n = (-4)^nΓ(2n+tfrac12)(sqrtπ n), large order a_n sim (-16)^nΓ(n+tfrac12)sqrtπ; Borel singularity at ζ = -116, the action of the nontrivial saddle of x^22 + g x^4.\n:exp - the exponential sum_k0 ħ^k  k (convergent sanity example).\n:painleve1 - the perturbative (0-instanton) series of Painlevé I in the Garoufalidis–Its–Kapaev–Mariño normalization -tfrac16 u + u^2 = z (arXiv:1002.3634, eq. 1.2): u_0(z) = sqrtzsum_n0 a_n z^-5n2 with a_0 = 1 and the exact rational recursion (eq. 2.1) a_n+1 = tfrac25n^2-148a_n - tfrac12 sum_m=1^n a_m a_n+1-m, giving a_1 = -148, a_2 = -494608, …. This returns the bare fluctuation sum_n a_n x^n (x = z^-52, power offset 0); the sqrtz prefactor and the transmonomial e^-kA z^54 (action A = 8sqrt35) are supplied by painleve1. Its Borel singularities sit at the instanton actions A.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.FormalSeries"},{"category":"method","location":"series/#Resurgence.Transseries-Tuple{Symbol, Integer}","page":"Series","text":"Transseries(name::Symbol, n::Integer; var = :ħ) -> Transseries{Rational{BigInt}}\n\nNamed course-canon transseries, each with n coefficients per sector:\n\n:euler - action A = 1, sectors Φ_0 = sum_k kħ^k+1 (the ħ  -ħ mirror of FormalSeries(:euler), placing the Borel pole at ζ = +1 = A on the Stokes ray θ = 0) and Φ_1 = 1; all higher sectors vanish identically. Stokes constant S_1 = -2πi (see stokes_constant).\n:airy - action A = -43 (the Borel singularity of the Ai sector; the relative weight e^-Aħ = e^4(3ħ) = e^2ξ is the Bi/Ai ratio), sectors Φ_0 = FormalSeries(:airy, n) and Φ_1 = FormalSeries(:airy_bi, n).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.Transseries"},{"category":"section","location":"errors/#Errors","page":"Errors","text":"Every invalid operation throws a typed error from the ResurgenceError hierarchy, each with an informative showerror. Structs are immutable and every operation returns a new object, so an error never leaves a half-mutated value behind.","title":"Errors"},{"category":"type","location":"errors/#Resurgence.DegeneratePade","page":"Errors","text":"DegeneratePade(L, M)\n\nThrown when the Toeplitz system of the [L/M] Padé approximant is singular - the underlying function admits a lower-degree rational representation. Request a lower order, or pass reduce = true to pade for automatic degree reduction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.DegeneratePade"},{"category":"type","location":"errors/#Resurgence.IncompatibleSeries","page":"Errors","text":"IncompatibleSeries(field, lhs, rhs)\n\nThrown by series arithmetic when the two operands disagree in field (e.g. :var, or a :power_offset mismatch that is not an integer shift).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.IncompatibleSeries"},{"category":"type","location":"errors/#Resurgence.InvalidArgument","page":"Errors","text":"InvalidArgument(msg)\n\nGeneric escape hatch for invalid inputs not covered by a more specific error type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.InvalidArgument"},{"category":"type","location":"errors/#Resurgence.NoSingularityFound","page":"Errors","text":"NoSingularityFound(msg)\n\nThrown when a Borel-plane singularity is required but none could be detected - e.g. conformal_borel or subtract_singularity on a series whose Padé approximant has no poles. Pass the singularity position explicitly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.NoSingularityFound"},{"category":"type","location":"errors/#Resurgence.PoleOnRay","page":"Errors","text":"PoleOnRay(pole, theta)\n\nThrown by Laplace summation when a Borel-plane pole lies (within tolerance) on the integration ray at angle theta - a Stokes ray. Use lateral_sum with side = :plus or :minus to integrate around it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.PoleOnRay"},{"category":"type","location":"errors/#Resurgence.ResurgenceError","page":"Errors","text":"ResurgenceError\n\nAbstract supertype of all typed errors thrown by Resurgence.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.ResurgenceError"},{"category":"type","location":"errors/#Resurgence.TransseriesSolveError","page":"Errors","text":"TransseriesSolveError(msg)\n\nThrown by transseries_solve and the Painlevé I layer when a transseries ODE cannot be solved as posed: an action that fails the ODE's leading-balance equation, a resonant sector whose kernel was not supplied (lattice = …), or a required mixed-charge Stokes datum the model must provide.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.TransseriesSolveError"},{"category":"section","location":"summation/#Summation","page":"Summation","text":"borel_sum and laplace_sum give Borel–Padé–Laplace sums, lateral_sum works on tilted rays, stokes_discontinuity is the jump across a Stokes line, and median_sum is the real average. A guard detects poles sitting on the integration ray instead of silently returning nonsense - see PoleOnRay.","title":"Summation"},{"category":"section","location":"summation/#Sequence-acceleration","page":"Summation","text":"accelerate offers Shanks, Wynn-ε, Levin-u/t and Richardson. These act on any sequence, not only on a Borel pipeline - partial_sums turns a series into one - so they are usable on their own as convergence accelerators.","title":"Sequence acceleration"},{"category":"section","location":"summation/#Hyperasymptotics","page":"Summation","text":"Optimal truncation, Dingle terminants, and level-one Berry–Howls hyperasymptotic summation.","title":"Hyperasymptotics"},{"category":"method","location":"summation/#Resurgence.borel_sum-Tuple{FormalSeries, Number}","page":"Summation","text":"borel_sum(Φ::FormalSeries, ħ; beta = power_offset(Φ), θ = 0, order = nothing,\n          rtol = eps^(3/4)) -> Complex\n\nFull Borel–Padé–Laplace pipeline convenience: laplace_sum(borel(Φ; beta), ħ; θ, order, rtol).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.borel_sum"},{"category":"method","location":"summation/#Resurgence.laplace_sum-Tuple{BorelSeries, Number}","page":"Summation","text":"laplace_sum(B::BorelSeries, ħ; θ = 0, order = nothing, rtol = eps^(3/4),\n            check_poles = true) -> Complex\n\nLaplace integral of the Borel transform along the ray arg ζ = θ:\n\nφ(ħ) = a_0 + frac1Γ(β) int_0^ e^iθ e^-ζħ ζ^β-1 r(ζ) dζ \n\nwhere r is the Padé approximant of the reduced Borel function (order is passed to pade) and a_0 the split-off constant term. The result's precision follows the type of ħ (pass a BigFloat for high precision). Throws PoleOnRay when a Padé pole lies on the integration ray - a Stokes ray; use lateral_sum there. Requires Re(e^iθħ)  0 for convergence.\n\nlaplace_sum(B::BorelSeries, r::AbstractBorelApproximant, ħ; θ = 0,\n            rtol = eps^(3/4), check_poles = true) -> Complex\n\nThe same integral with an explicit (possibly prebuilt or non-Padé) approximant r of the reduced Borel function - the seam every AbstractBorelApproximant plugs into, e.g. laplace_sum(B, conformal_borel(B), ħ).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.laplace_sum"},{"category":"method","location":"summation/#Resurgence.lateral_sum-Tuple{BorelSeries, Number}","page":"Summation","text":"lateral_sum(B::BorelSeries, ħ; θ = 0, side = :plus, tilt = 1//100,\n            order = nothing, rtol = eps^(3/4)) -> Complex\n\nLateral Borel sum on a Stokes ray: integrates along the tilted ray θ  mathrmtilt (side = :plus above, :minus below). Because the Padé approximant is meromorphic, the value is independent of tilt as long as no pole lies between the tilted ray and θ - it is the lateral sum, not an approximation to it. The lateral_sum(B, r::AbstractBorelApproximant, ħ; …) method takes an explicit approximant, like laplace_sum.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.lateral_sum"},{"category":"method","location":"summation/#Resurgence.stokes_discontinuity-Tuple{BorelSeries, Number}","page":"Summation","text":"stokes_discontinuity(B::BorelSeries, ħ; θ = 0, kwargs...) -> Complex\n\nThe Stokes discontinuity across the ray arg ζ = θ: mathrmdisc_θ = s_θ^+(ħ) - s_θ^-(ħ), the difference of the two lateral_sums. Sign convention (pinned by the Euler oracle test): for Φ = Σ_n nħ^n+1 (Borel pole at ζ = 1) and real ħ  0, stokes_discontinuity(borel(Φ), ħ) ≈ +2πi\\, e^{-1/ħ}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.stokes_discontinuity"},{"category":"method","location":"summation/#Resurgence.accelerate-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"Summation","text":"accelerate(seq::AbstractVector; method = :wynn, order = nothing) -> Number\n\nAccelerate the convergence of the sequence seq (element seq[n] is s_n) and return the extrapolated limit. Available methods:\n\n:richardson - delegates to richardson (order sweeps, default min(4, length - 1)); best for sequences with 1n power-law error.\n:shanks - order iterated Shanks transformations S(s)_n = (s_n+1 s_n-1 - s_n^2)(s_n+1 + s_n-1 - 2 s_n) (default: as many as the length allows); exact on s_n = s + α q^n.\n:wynn - Wynn's ε-algorithm, descending to even column ε_2order (default: the deepest reachable even column); an efficient equivalent of iterated Shanks, robust for alternating and divergent-series partial sums.\n:levin_u, :levin_t - Levin transforms with β = 1 and remainder estimates ω_n = (n+1)Δs_n-1 (u) or ω_n = Δs_n-1 (t), using order + 2 sequence elements (default: all); typically the fastest accelerator for both convergent and alternating divergent series.\n\nPrecision follows the element type (Rational input is promoted to BigFloat); complex sequences work. See partial_sums for building the input from a FormalSeries.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.accelerate"},{"category":"method","location":"summation/#Resurgence.partial_sums-Tuple{FormalSeries, Any}","page":"Summation","text":"partial_sums(Φ::FormalSeries, x) -> Vector\n\nThe partial sums s_k = sum_n=0^k a_n x^p_n of the series evaluated at x, where p_n = n + mathrmpower_offset - the natural input for accelerate, both for convergent series and for divergent ones (where the Levin/Wynn transforms act as summation methods).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.partial_sums"},{"category":"method","location":"summation/#Resurgence.dingle_terminant-Tuple{Real, Number}","page":"Summation","text":"dingle_terminant(ν, x; side = :plus, rtol = eps^(3/4)) -> Complex\n\nThe (basic, Dingle-normalized) terminant - the Borel sum of Σ_k0 Γ(ν+k)x^k:\n\nΛ_ν(x) = int_0^ t^ν-1 e^-t fracdt1 - x t  qquad ν  0 \n\nevaluated by quadrature in the precision of x. Λ_ν(0) = Γ(ν). For x on the positive real axis the pole t = 1x sits on the contour (the Stokes case): the ray is tilted by side (:plus above, :minus below - lateral terminants), and the two sides differ by the residue jump Λ_ν^+(x) - Λ_ν^-(x) = 2πi x^-ν e^-1x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.dingle_terminant"},{"category":"method","location":"summation/#Resurgence.hyper_sum-Tuple{FormalSeries, Number}","page":"Summation","text":"hyper_sum(Φ::FormalSeries, ħ; adjacent, action, stokes, N = nothing,\n          M = nothing, side = :plus, rtol = eps^(3/4)) -> Complex\n\nLevel-1 hyperasymptotics (Berry–Howls): truncate Φ at N_0 terms and re-expand the remainder through the adjacent sector Φ_1 = Σ_m a^(1)_m ħ^m+β_1 at the dominant action A with Stokes constant stokes = S_1 (this package's convention: S_1 = 2πimathrmRes for a simple Borel pole, see stokes_constant), using dingle_terminants:\n\nφ(ħ)  sum_nN_0 a_n ħ^p_n - fracS_12πi(ħA)^p_N_0\n       sum_mM a^(1)_m A^m+β_1 Λ_p_N_0-m-β_1(ħA) \n\nDefaults: N_0 from optimal_truncation(Φ, ħ; rule = :action, A = action); the terminant series is truncated at its own smallest term (or at M, or when the terminant order ν would leave the ν  0 domain). For Euler-type simple poles the adjacent sector is a constant and the re-expansion is exact; the error of the level-1 result improves on the superasymptotic e^-Aħ by the documented Berry–Howls factor. side selects the lateral terminant on Stokes rays (ħA positive real).\n\nPass plain series data - extract sectors from a Transseries yourself (sector(F, 1), action(F)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.hyper_sum"},{"category":"method","location":"summation/#Resurgence.optimal_truncation-Tuple{FormalSeries, Number}","page":"Summation","text":"optimal_truncation(Φ::FormalSeries, ħ; rule = :smallest_term, A = nothing)\n    -> NamedTuple{(:N, :value, :error)}\n\nSuperasymptotics: truncate the divergent series at its smallest term. Returns the number of kept terms N^* (so value = Σ_nN^* a_n ħ^p_n) and the magnitude of the first omitted term as error - the classic sim e^-Aħ superasymptotic error estimate. Rules:\n\n:smallest_term (default) - N^* = argmin_n a_n ħ^p_n over the stored range (if the terms are still shrinking at the truncation order, the stored range is simply exhausted);\n:action - N^* = mathrmround(Aħ) - mathrmpower_offset clamped to the stored range, with the action modulus A taken from the keyword or from large_order_fit.\n\nQuadrature-free.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.optimal_truncation"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#Plotting","page":"Plotting","text":"The core depends only on QuadGK, PrecompileTools and the LinearAlgebra stdlib. Plotting and AAA rational approximation live in package extensions, loaded on demand - calling one of these before its trigger package is loaded raises an error telling you what to load.\n\nusing CairoMakie                   # or GLMakie\nplot_borel_plane(borel(Φ))         # pole scatter with Laplace rays\nplot_large_order(Φ)\nplot_optimal_truncation(Φ, 0.1)\n\nusing BaryRational\naaa_borel(borel(Φ))                # AAA rational approximation of the Borel function","title":"Plotting"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#Makie-extension","page":"Plotting","text":"","title":"Makie extension"},{"category":"section","location":"plotting/#BaryRational-extension","page":"Plotting","text":"AAA runs in Float64 - treat it as a pole and branch-cut hunter, not a high-precision summation tool (that is Padé's job, in BigFloat).","title":"BaryRational extension"},{"category":"function","location":"plotting/#Resurgence.plot_borel_plane","page":"Plotting","text":"plot_borel_plane(B::BorelSeries; order = nothing, rays = [0.0], kwargs...)\n\nScatter the Borel–Padé poles of B in the complex ζ-plane with the Laplace rays overlaid. Implemented in the Makie package extension - load a Makie backend (e.g. using CairoMakie) first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.plot_borel_plane"},{"category":"function","location":"plotting/#Resurgence.plot_large_order","page":"Plotting","text":"plot_large_order(Φ::FormalSeries; kwargs...)\n\nRatio-test diagnostic for the factorial growth of Φ: the sequence |aₙ/aₙ₋₁| / n tends to 1/A, where A is the distance to the nearest Borel singularity. Overlays the asymptote from large_order_fit. Implemented in the Makie package extension - load a backend (using CairoMakie) first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.plot_large_order"},{"category":"function","location":"plotting/#Resurgence.plot_optimal_truncation","page":"Plotting","text":"plot_optimal_truncation(Φ::FormalSeries, ħ; kwargs...)\nplot_optimal_truncation(Φ::FormalSeries, ħs::AbstractVector; kwargs...)\n\nThe superasymptotic error curve: term magnitudes |aₙ ħ^{pₙ}| versus truncation order N (log scale), with the optimal-truncation minimum N⋆ from optimal_truncation marked. Passing a vector of couplings overlays one U-curve per ħ. Implemented in the Makie package extension - load a backend (using CairoMakie) first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.plot_optimal_truncation"},{"category":"function","location":"plotting/#Resurgence.aaa_approximant","page":"Plotting","text":"aaa_approximant(zs, fs; tol = 1e-13, mmax = 100, var = :ζ) -> AAAApproximant\naaa_borel(B::BorelSeries; samples = 128, radius = nothing, tol = 1e-13,\n          mmax = 100) -> AAAApproximant\n\nAAA rational approximation of the reduced Borel function - an alternative pole/branch-cut hunter, robust where equispaced-coefficient Padé struggles. Implemented in the BaryRational package extension: run using BaryRational first. aaa_approximant fits samples (zs, fs) directly; aaa_borel samples the truncated reduced Borel sum of B on disks inside its radius of convergence. The result is an AbstractBorelApproximant: it plugs into laplace_sum(B, aaa_borel(B), ħ) and supports poles/residues.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.aaa_approximant"},{"category":"function","location":"plotting/#Resurgence.aaa_borel","page":"Plotting","text":"aaa_approximant(zs, fs; tol = 1e-13, mmax = 100, var = :ζ) -> AAAApproximant\naaa_borel(B::BorelSeries; samples = 128, radius = nothing, tol = 1e-13,\n          mmax = 100) -> AAAApproximant\n\nAAA rational approximation of the reduced Borel function - an alternative pole/branch-cut hunter, robust where equispaced-coefficient Padé struggles. Implemented in the BaryRational package extension: run using BaryRational first. aaa_approximant fits samples (zs, fs) directly; aaa_borel samples the truncated reduced Borel sum of B on disks inside its radius of convergence. The result is an AbstractBorelApproximant: it plugs into laplace_sum(B, aaa_borel(B), ħ) and supports poles/residues.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.aaa_borel"},{"category":"section","location":"alien/#Alien-calculus","page":"Alien calculus","text":"alien_derivative via the bridge equation (single- and multi-parameter, by lattice charge or by singularity location), stokes_automorphism as both a sigma-shift and an operator, and stokes_constant from either Borel-plane residues or large-order growth.\n\nThe single- and multi-action routines share their interface - the functions below dispatch on both Transseries and MultiTransseries.","title":"Alien calculus"},{"category":"function","location":"alien/#Resurgence.alien_derivative","page":"Alien calculus","text":"alien_derivative(F::Transseries, l::Integer = 1; stokes) -> Transseries\n\nThe alien derivative Δ_lA of a one-parameter transseries via the bridge equation dotΔ_lA F = S_l σ^1-l _σ F, which sector-wise reads\n\nΔ_lA Φ_m = S_l (m + l) Φ_m+l \n\nstokes is the Stokes constant S_1 (a number, for l = 1) or a vector (S_1 S_2 ). The result has n_sectors(F) - l sectors; when l ≥ n_sectors(F) every visible sector is annihilated and the zero transseries (one zero sector) is returned.\n\nExample\n\nF = Transseries(:euler, 20)\nalien_derivative(F; stokes = -2π*im)     # Δ_A F: single sector S₁·Φ₁ = -2πi\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.alien_derivative"},{"category":"method","location":"alien/#Resurgence.alien_derivative-Union{Tuple{K}, Tuple{A}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{MultiTransseries{T, A, K}, NTuple{K, Integer}}} where {T, A, K}","page":"Alien calculus","text":"alien_derivative(F::MultiTransseries, ℓ = e_1; stokes, multiplicity = nothing)\n    -> MultiTransseries\n\nThe alien derivative Δ_ℓA at integer lattice charge ℓ (an NTuple{K}), via the general bridge equation Δ_ℓA Φ_n = S_ℓ μ_ℓ(n) Φ_n+ℓ. Weight-lowering: the result sector at n reads the input sector at n+ℓ (dropped when n ⋡ 0), so the result carries the exponential weights shifted down by ℓ.\n\nstokes supplies S_ℓ - a scalar (used for every charge), a Dict ℓ ↦ S_ℓ, or a callable ℓ -> S_ℓ. For a single-direction charge ℓ = c·e_j the multiplicity μ_ℓ(n) = n_j + c is used automatically; a mixed charge (≥ 2 nonzero components) is model-dependent and requires an explicit multiplicity = (n, ℓ) -> Number.\n\nPassing a Vector of charges composes the derivatives Δ_ℓ_r    Δ_ℓ_1 (first element applied innermost). Reduces to the one-parameter alien_derivative under the rank-1 embedding.\n\nΔ_ω is ℂlog ħ-linear - log ħ has no Borel singularity away from ζ = 0, so Δ_ω(log ħ) = 0 - hence this acts blockwise on resonant sectors and needs no special casing for them. To index by singularity location rather than charge, see alien_derivative(F, ω::Number).\n\nnote: Why the mixed-charge multiplicity is model-dependent\nResonance is the reason, and it does not go away. When the lattice has a kernel vector k (see resonance_lattice), the kernel monomial - σ₁σ₂ for A = (A, -A) - has weight zero, so it can appear to any power without violating the weight grading. The bridge equation then admits an unconstrained tower Δ_ω F = sum_j S_ℓ_0 + jk(σ₁σ₂)^j(textderivation) of independent Stokes data - for Painlevé I these are exactly the extra Stokes constants. No universal formula can pick them; supply multiplicity from your model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.alien_derivative"},{"category":"method","location":"alien/#Resurgence.alien_derivative-Union{Tuple{K}, Tuple{A}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{MultiTransseries{T, A, K}, Number}} where {T, A, K}","page":"Alien calculus","text":"alien_derivative(F::MultiTransseries, ω::Number; stokes, multiplicity = nothing)\n    -> MultiTransseries\n\nThe alien derivative at a singularity location ω in the Borel plane, as opposed to at a lattice charge: the sum of Δ_{ℓ·A} over the whole fiber charges(F, ω) = ℓ  0  ℓA = ω inside the support's bounding box.\n\nNote the argument-type distinction, which is the whole point of this method: an NTuple is a charge, a Number is a location. (The one-parameter alien_derivative(::Transseries, l::Integer) indexes charges, not locations.) At a non-resonant lattice the fiber is a single charge and the two agree; at a resonant one the fiber is a tower - for A = (A, -A), ω = A collects (1,0), (2,1), (3,2), … - and each charge carries its own Stokes constant, so pass stokes as a Dict or callable.\n\nThrows InvalidArgument when no charge in the box lies over ω.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.alien_derivative"},{"category":"method","location":"alien/#Resurgence.median_sum-Tuple{FormalSeries, Number}","page":"Alien calculus","text":"median_sum(Φ::FormalSeries, ħ; beta = power_offset(Φ), θ = 0, kwargs...) -> Complex\n\nMedian summation of a single series: the average of the two lateral sums, (s_+(Φ) + s_-(Φ))2. This is the leading-sector case of the transseries method (they agree exactly when the one-instanton sector is constant and higher sectors vanish, e.g. Euler); real on the ray for real coefficients and real ħ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.median_sum"},{"category":"method","location":"alien/#Resurgence.median_sum-Tuple{Transseries, Number, Number}","page":"Alien calculus","text":"median_sum(F::Transseries, σ, ħ; stokes, θ = 0, kwargs...) -> Complex\n\nMedian summation of a transseries: the lateral sum at the half-shifted parameter, s_mathrmmed(F)(σ) = s_+(F)(σ + S_12) - halfway (in the sense of mathfrakS^12) between the two lateral representatives, so s_-(F)(σ - S_12) gives the same value. For real resurgent data (real coefficients, real σ, real ħ on the Stokes ray) the median sum is real.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.median_sum"},{"category":"method","location":"alien/#Resurgence.median_sum-Union{Tuple{K}, Tuple{A}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{MultiTransseries{T, A, K}, Tuple, Number}} where {T, A, K}","page":"Alien calculus","text":"median_sum(F::MultiTransseries, σ, ħ; stokes, θ = 0, kwargs...) -> Complex\n\nMedian summation of a K-parameter transseries: the lateral sum at the per-direction half-shifted parameter σ_j + S_e_j2 on the ray θ. Real for real resurgent data (real coefficients, real σ, real ħ), as in the one-parameter median_sum.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.median_sum"},{"category":"method","location":"alien/#Resurgence.pointed_alien_derivative-Union{Tuple{K}, Tuple{A}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{MultiTransseries{T, A, K}, NTuple{K, Integer}}} where {T, A, K}","page":"Alien calculus","text":"pointed_alien_derivative(F::MultiTransseries, ℓ = e_1; stokes, multiplicity = nothing)\n    -> MultiTransseries\n\nThe pointed alien derivative dotΔ_ℓA = e^-(ℓA)ħ Δ_ℓA - weight-preserving (the reintroduced weight e^{-(ℓ·A)/ħ} cancels the lowering, so a sector at n+ℓ maps back to n+ℓ). These are the building blocks of the Stokes automorphism exp(Σ_ω Δ̇_ω) and commute for distinct fundamental directions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.pointed_alien_derivative"},{"category":"method","location":"alien/#Resurgence.stokes_automorphism-Tuple{Transseries, Number}","page":"Alien calculus","text":"stokes_automorphism(F::Transseries, σ::Number; stokes) -> Number\n\nThe Stokes automorphism mathfrakS = exp(sum_l1 dotΔ_lA) acting on a one-parameter transseries. By the bridge equation it exponentiates to the parameter shift σ  σ + S_1: the two lateral sums of the same transseries represent the same function at shifted parameters,\n\ns_-(F)(σ) = s_+(F)(σ + S_1) \n\nwhich is the connection formula across the Stokes ray (pinned by the Euler oracle test). This function returns the shifted parameter σ + S₁; apply it between side = :minus and side = :plus calls of transseries_sum.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.stokes_automorphism"},{"category":"method","location":"alien/#Resurgence.stokes_automorphism-Union{Tuple{K}, Tuple{A}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{MultiTransseries{T, A, K}, Tuple}} where {T, A, K}","page":"Alien calculus","text":"stokes_automorphism(F::MultiTransseries, σ; stokes, θ = 0) -> NTuple\n\nThe Stokes automorphism as the parameter shift it induces: for each fundamental direction j whose action A_j lies on the ray θ, σ_j ↦ σ_j + S_{e_j}; directions off the ray are unchanged. The shift S_{e_j} is the leading Stokes constant of the one-instanton sector in direction j (equivalently the leading coefficient of pointed_alien_derivative(F, e_j)), so the σ-shift is a derived consequence of the alien machinery rather than a definition. At K = 1 this returns σ + S₁, reproducing the M2 connection formula s₋(F)(σ) = s₊(F)(σ + S₁).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.stokes_automorphism"},{"category":"method","location":"alien/#Resurgence.stokes_automorphism-Union{Tuple{MultiTransseries{T, A, K}}, Tuple{K}, Tuple{A}, Tuple{T}} where {T, A, K}","page":"Alien calculus","text":"stokes_automorphism(F::MultiTransseries; stokes, θ = 0, order = nothing)\n    -> MultiTransseries\n\nThe Stokes automorphism as an operator mathfrakS_θ = expbig(sum_j S_e_j Δ_e_jAbig) = sum_m0 tfrac1m mathcal N^m F with generator mathcal N = sum_j S_e_j Δ_e_jA over the fundamental directions on the ray θ. The plain alien derivatives lower the total weight, so mathcal N is nilpotent on the bounded support and the series terminates (order caps it if given). This is the closed derivation-algebra object on MultiTransseries; the summed action of its exponential is the parameter shift of the other method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.stokes_automorphism"},{"category":"method","location":"alien/#Resurgence.stokes_constant-Tuple{BorelSeries}","page":"Alien calculus","text":"stokes_constant(B::BorelSeries; location = nothing, order = nothing) -> Complex\n\nStokes constant from the Borel plane: S = 2πi  mathrmRes_ζ=ζ_0 hatB(ζ) at the Padé pole ζ₀ nearest location (default: the pole closest to the origin, the leading singularity). The residue of the full Borel transform includes the ζ^β-1Γ(β) prefactor split off by the rising-factorial normalization.\n\nSign convention (pinned by the Euler oracle): for Φ_0 = Σ_k kħ^k+1 the Borel transform is 1(1-ζ) with residue -1 at ζ = A = 1, so S_1 = -2πi, coherent with stokes_discontinuity via mathrmdisc = s_+ - s_- = -S_1 e^-Aħ s(Φ_1).\n\nExact (to working precision) for simple poles, i.e. when the one-instanton sector is a constant. At a branch point (a nontrivial Φ_1, e.g. Airy) the Padé pole string emulates the cut and the nearest residue does not converge to S_1 - use the large-order method stokes_constant(Φ::FormalSeries) there.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.stokes_constant"},{"category":"method","location":"alien/#Resurgence.stokes_constant-Tuple{FormalSeries}","page":"Alien calculus","text":"stokes_constant(Φ::FormalSeries; order = 4) -> Real\n\nModulus of the Stokes constant from coefficient asymptotics: the resurgence large-order relation a_n sim fracS_12πi fracΓ(p_n+b)A^p_n+b (c_0^(1) + O(1n)) gives, for a one-instanton sector normalized to c_0^(1) = 1,\n\nS_1 = 2π S_mathrmfit A^b \n\nwith (A b S_mathrmfit) from large_order_fit (order sweeps of Richardson extrapolation). Valid at branch points where the pole-residue method is not (the phase of S_1 is not visible in coefficient moduli; it follows from the direction of the singularity - e.g. purely imaginary for real coefficients).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.stokes_constant"},{"category":"section","location":"#Resurgence.jl","page":"Home","text":"Resurgence - divergent and asymptotic series made usable: Borel transforms, Padé approximants, Borel–Padé–Laplace summation, sequence acceleration, large-order analysis, transseries and alien calculus.\n\nA series whose coefficients grow factorially has zero radius of convergence, so it is not a function - but it still determines one, and it carries more information than a convergent series would. Such expansions are the normal output of any problem where a small parameter enters singularly: singular perturbation and boundary-layer problems, semiclassical and WKB expansions, nonlinear ODEs near an irregular singular point, the coefficient asymptotics of generating functions, saddle-point expansions of integrals, and perturbation theory in quantum mechanics and quantum field theory.\n\nResurgence is the theory that makes such a series usable - Écalle's framework, and the one this package implements. Its basic tool is the Borel transform: for Phi(hbar) = sum_n a_n hbar^n+beta,\n\nhatB(zeta) = sum_n fraca_nGamma(n+beta) zeta^n+beta-1\n\nconverges on a disk, and the Laplace integral int_0^infty e^-zetahbarhatB(zeta)dzeta resums the original series to an actual number.\n\nWhat makes this more than a summation trick is the geometry of the Borel plane. Each singularity of hat B sits at some A, and that one location does three things at once: it fixes the growth a_n sim SGamma(n+b)A^n of the coefficients, it marks an exponentially small scale e^-Ahbar that no order of the series can see, and - when it lies on the integration ray - it makes the two lateral sums differ by exactly such a term, the Stokes phenomenon. Divergence is therefore diagnostic: the growth rate of coefficients you already have tells you about behaviour beyond all orders. In a physics problem the A are instanton actions; in an ODE they are the exponents of the subdominant solutions; in coefficient asymptotics they are the dominant singularities. The machinery does not care which.\n\nTwo things you can do with a list of coefficients:\n\nget a number - borel_sum / median_sum for a resummed value, accelerate for sequence acceleration, hyper_sum when optimal truncation is not accurate enough;\nget structure - large_order_fit and borel_pade_poles recover the singularity locations, exponents and Stokes constants from the coefficients alone.\n\nSeries with exact coefficients stay exact: Rational input runs through the whole pipeline, and precision follows the caller's argument type, so the same code runs in Float64 or in BigFloat at whatever setprecision is active.","title":"Resurgence.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Scope","page":"Home","text":"This is an analysis engine for asymptotic series however you produced them - by hand, from a recursion, from a symbolic computation, or read in from a file. It is not a coefficient generator for any particular model: the handful of built-in FormalSeries(:name, n) recursions exist as test oracles and worked examples, not as a library of expansions.","title":"Scope"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"From the Julia REPL:\n\npkg> add Resurgence\n\nRequires Julia 1.10 or later.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"using Resurgence\n\n# The Euler series, exact rationals; divergent for every ħ ≠ 0\nΦ = FormalSeries(:euler, 40)\n\n# Borel transform, exactly −1/(1+ζ)\nB = borel(Φ)\npoles(pade(B; order = 1))         # ζ = −1, the Borel singularity\n\n# Borel–Padé–Laplace summation; precision follows the argument type\nborel_sum(Φ, 0.1; order = 1)      # ≈ −0.0915633, i.e. −e^{10}E₁(10)\nborel_sum(Φ, big\"0.1\"; order = 1) # same pipeline in BigFloat\n\n# Large-order analysis: aₙ ~ S·Γ(n+b)/Aⁿ\nlarge_order_fit(Φ)                # (A, b, S) = (1, 0, 1) for Euler\n\n# Stokes phenomenon: lateral sums, discontinuity, median summation\nΨ = FormalSeries(:airy_bi, 40)    # Borel pole at +4/3, so ℝ₊ is a Stokes ray\nlateral_sum(borel(Ψ), 0.3; side = :plus)   # complex\nmedian_sum(Ψ, 0.3)                         # real\n\n# Transseries and alien calculus\nF = Transseries(:euler, 20)\nalien_derivative(F; stokes = -2π * im)","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"#A-note-on-name-collisions","page":"Home","text":"Three exports - coefficients, derivative, evaluate - are also exported by AbstractAlgebra.jl and by several other computer-algebra packages. They are the right names for what they do, so they are kept; if you load both packages into one namespace, import explicitly rather than relying on using:\n\nusing Resurgence: FormalSeries, borel, coefficients\nimport AbstractAlgebra as AA","title":"A note on name collisions"},{"category":"section","location":"#Related-packages","page":"Home","text":"This package is self-contained and depends on nothing below. It is also one foundation of a family of Julia packages for exact and asymptotic methods - where a discrete, exactly computable structure controls a continuous, only-asymptotically-defined one: ClusterAlgebras.jl is the discrete side, and ExactWKB.jl is the bridge, which Borel sums the WKB series of a Schrödinger-type ODE with this package and matches the result against cluster combinatorics via the Iwaki–Nakanishi dictionary.","title":"Related packages"},{"category":"module","location":"#Resurgence.Resurgence","page":"Home","text":"Resurgence\n\nFoundation package for resurgence theory: generic formal power series, exact Borel transforms, Padé approximants over any field, Borel–Padé–Laplace summation with lateral sums and Stokes discontinuities, and large-order analysis. Deliberately free of any cluster-algebra or WKB dependency - that bridge lives in ExactWKB.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Resurgence.Resurgence"}]
}
